{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1445 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "22c4f64e1310c6e9786a2a5ffd59d099926306ebd95097fa2afbd71a3e179224" :edb/db-before-t 1444 :edb/db-after-t 1445 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534757 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:59:59.778Z" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198082 1145 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS= \\Big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z,\\, y\\in \\mathbb N\\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$.\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{(x, {\\color{red}{1}}) \\:: \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/8f5aa73042b1d2acceb1bb3905a19383.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\Big\\{(x,{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\:: \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_2$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/61f4160522933e19954c880409db96e5.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3,S_4,S_5,\\ldots$, similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= S_{1}∪S_{2}∪S_{3}∪S_{4}∪… \\\\\n&= ⋃_(i ∈ N)S_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/0f3b930634c622930def542df33bddf4.png)" 13194139534757 false] [17592186198082 1145 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\n\\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z,\\, y\\in \\mathbb N\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}.$\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{ (x, {\\color{red}{1}}) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/6b63e973bbcc3e25d601a1f6cef97f5a00cc22e738c46d04a1ac06851421894e.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\Big\\{ (x,{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_2$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f24ce8f7610d096c795a8a74b0b714b720c36442042c28b66432d6b2102fb9d4.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3,S_4,S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup {S}_{4}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c999c61d8ffbb27d47272811e32340cef3253b4730e684be10615487a8b27200.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198097 1145 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2\\} \\times (0,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1)$.\n\n$$\nS= \\Big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: x\\in \\{{\\color{red}{1}},{\\color{blue}{2}}\\},\\, y\\in (0,1) \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1)$.\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{({\\color{red}{1}},y) \\:: \\: y \\in(0,1) \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/3b80da3b101386f2d3a40a0bf941e20d.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1)$.\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{({\\color{blue}{2}},y) \\:: \\: y \\in(0,1) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/b5e4c38cb65a7cd28c8770c6ffe1f66d.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS = S_1\\cup S_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/01bdb54466facecd2edb472318568c45.png)" 13194139534757 false] [17592186198097 1145 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2\\} \\times (0,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in \\{{\\color{red}{1}},{\\color{blue}{2}}\\},\\, y\\in (0,1) \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{ ({\\color{red}{1}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in(0,1) \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/56f200fce557749a56b9b5b51bc670da5f8c7f2cdb0196392ee80efcd6be980a.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{2}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in(0,1) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f96074cc265bba6eb59717364d2aa5390fdccea4d398fe5ae0b99f7ad82f0b93.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS = S_1\\cup S_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/0c0529aaa680b4b572d17f2cfbfb76d805134e72c50ab3ed469286cc025185a3.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198133 1100 "Sketch a representation of the set $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane given that $I = [-1,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, \\, i^2].$" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198133 1100 "Sketch a representation of the set $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane given that $I = [-1,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = \\{i \\} \\times [0, \\, i^2]$." 13194139534757 false] [17592186198133 1145 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{-0.5} = \\{ -0.5 \\} \\times [0, 0.25] = \\big\\{ (-0.5,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, 0.25] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5, 0)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.25)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/ccc75eac899efcab1d0b6fde12609a3ea8f61d6fec74c739ef3393fad32645a9.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, i^2] = \\big\\{ (i,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, i^2] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i, i^2).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/60c98be5d97748e9285bb5e9c30bba9781a5ef99245789718a7828b18a6df8fb.png)\n\n \n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b3a61d686c78c76a4f461c1c771437d31030339b634661da8b8dc4f492db55eb.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198133 1145 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x \\:: \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5$, we have\n\n$$\nX_{-0.5} = \\{-0.5 \\} \\times [0, 0.25] = \\big\\{(-0.5,k) \\:: \\: k \\in [0, 0.25] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5, 0)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.25)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/39d7728d4b077bbadefe0f4e78ccdac8.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{i \\} \\times [0, i^2] = \\big\\{(i,k) \\:: \\: k \\in [0, i^2] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i, i^2)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/8b4e8f9f69ee8255a65b8604ebb5d208.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I$, these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/a61ed7144cb09c3bb8d7f03cae547e73.png)" 13194139534757 false] [17592186198134 1100 "Let $I = [0,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1]$. Sketch a representation of $\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane." 13194139534757 false] [17592186198134 1100 "Let $I = [0,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1].$ Sketch a representation of $\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane." 13194139534757 true] [17592186198134 1145 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for all}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{0.5} = [-0.5,0] \\times [-1, 1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-0.5, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-0.5, -1),$ $(-0.5, 1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ad650cb3f2fa4da920a442e8cc1b24af5027595cba4e43138eff2126cf4c102.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-i, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1),$ $(-i,1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a37cfa508cf3a60536084fbc1e57c5aeac3414904d2ad72ef1deda85dc4eddd8.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5e9a611629f4370c57e77a057439c759670afa17c14c4b2339e1d471f616ea72.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198134 1145 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x \\:: \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for all}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5$, we have\n\n$$\nX_{0.5} = [-0.5,0] \\times [-1, 1] = \\big\\{(a,b) \\:: \\: a \\in [-0.5, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-0.5, -1)$, $(-0.5, 1)$, $(0, 1)$, and $(0, -1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/ddb9d22d2183ec9959a2da585dedfc81.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1] = \\big\\{(a,b) \\:: \\: a \\in [-i, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1)$, $(-i,1)$, $(0, 1)$, and $(0, -1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/64febcdf1b6f8c3dd38c61e6e1269688.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/58f000d1c247eea66c409933d93cf088.png)" 13194139534757 false] [17592186198199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307974 13194139534757 true] [17592186198199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308009 13194139534757 true] [17592186198199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308016 13194139534757 true] [17592186198200 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307960 13194139534757 true] [17592186198200 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307967 13194139534757 true] [17592186198200 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307981 13194139534757 true] [17592186198200 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308002 13194139534757 true] [17592186198201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307953 13194139534757 true] [17592186198201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307988 13194139534757 true] [17592186198201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307995 13194139534757 true] [17592186198201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308023 13194139534757 true] [17592186198201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308030 13194139534757 true] [17592186198202 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307939 13194139534757 true] [17592186198202 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307946 13194139534757 true] [17592186198202 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308037 13194139534757 true] [17592186198202 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308044 13194139534757 true] [17592186198644 1017 #edb/ref 17592186194760 13194139534757 false] [17592186198644 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307933 13194139534757 true] [17592186198644 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534757 true] [17592186198644 1100 "Let $I = [0, 2]⊂R$ and $X_{i} = {i} \\cdot [0, i]$. Which of the following represents $⋂_(i ∈ I)X_{i}$?" 13194139534757 false] [17592186198644 1100 "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198644 1145 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [0,1]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139534757 true] [17592186198705 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534757 true] [17592186198705 1100 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S = R \\cdot N$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534757 false] [17592186198705 1100 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times N$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198705 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198729 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534757 true] [17592186198729 1100 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S = {0, 1, 2} \\cdot {-1, 1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534757 false] [17592186198729 1100 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198729 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(0,-1),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,1),(2,-1),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c5fdae9b803df3873ee7deef4a36678e0d8274dd256fe331360261f1fe6dd492.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198734 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534757 true] [17592186198734 1100 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S = {1, 2} \\cdot (-1, 1)$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534757 false] [17592186198734 1100 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={1,2}\\times (-1,1)$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534757 true] [17592186198734 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={1,2}\\times (-1,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {1,2},\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/11522acdfeaad022847fedbcf6052424216587f99f2691e80ed96152f30818a4.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/90b6a9c00d366e4a03c8c974476fdf06bc9fa73c9c26e9a0cd6ba9a4bda8e66d.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/bbf0d5ecc57bc30c7a3f4db7a1d30b27633e4586b2768e315715df72ce41333f.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307934 13194139534757 true] [17592186307933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307935 13194139534757 true] [17592186307933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307936 13194139534757 true] [17592186307933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307937 13194139534757 true] [17592186307933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307938 13194139534757 true] [17592186307933 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534757 true] [17592186307933 1041 "selection" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307933 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307934 13194139534757 true] [17592186307933 1110 #uuid "dda8c1e1-bad9-5b1c-b928-a0d2962fffd5" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307934 1034 "$∅,$ the empty set" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307934 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534757 true] [17592186307934 1111 #uuid "132f77bd-9ddf-5caf-96a1-e220936f45d0" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307935 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/df4a1ecffe6302ee57250944ec8f178f46a066b2edff2ed1afb200e7b0217cf7.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307935 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307935 1111 #uuid "098babed-40a5-5df9-8de1-0a88c19800f4" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307936 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/6b5b39546eabacdf948bd87e793727fdc8cd8f2664df037be53bfb630fc41670.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307936 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307936 1111 #uuid "715d6dad-5b8d-5a07-ab6f-cc9c2db51162" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307937 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/506d86848903de9f038d541152aabf67d99d0f4d0a7e5f5772b811ff1cf9dade.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307937 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307937 1111 #uuid "280a367a-db86-510d-83e6-8f1354018cdd" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307938 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Ac