6111140b07556432b5240fa5a9efacd33ce19989.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307945 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307945 1111 #uuid "1e664514-cba7-564e-8f55-c3cbd248245f" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307946 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307947 13194139534757 true] [17592186307946 1089 "q-175302" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307946 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534757 true] [17592186307946 1100 "Let $I=[1,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times {i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307946 1113 #uuid "ccdf2b91-4206-5a3f-b15f-21feb13a3062" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307946 1145 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,2-1]\\times {1}=[0,1]\\times {1}={(k,1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/ddb7039dfcdbc2ef4070ce732c635a48194b6675cc3c78de6267e656c5e8387a.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times {i}={(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,2-i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(2-i,i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/e83644a2f0de96ee58ea893028bb7a42681ee71a15d194f2b344c4c15f60fafe.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139534757 true] [17592186307947 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307948 13194139534757 true] [17592186307947 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307949 13194139534757 true] [17592186307947 1000 #edb/ref 1759218