 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307952 1111 #uuid "9f5a2d4d-e28d-521e-867c-0d244f5b21b4" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307953 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307954 13194139534757 true] [17592186307953 1089 "q-177652" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307953 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534757 true] [17592186307953 1100 "Let $I=[-1,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307953 1113 #uuid "15afbaad-5b0e-58d9-8df7-ceeef530333d" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307953 1145 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{-0.5}={-0.5}\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(-0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5,-0.5)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b7793d85e02ccd05c55c34971c90be087d58274eabd21f46fe09f08ccd60797b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-|i|,|i|]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-|i|)$ to the point $(i,|i|).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c30712295adfe51c655662cc09f8f1e23b3ea97573693154e0e0b6ba48e15c07.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307955 13194139534757 true] [17592186307954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307956 13194139534757 true] [17592186307954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307957 13194139534757 true] [17592186307954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307958 13194139534757 true] [17592186307954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307959 13194139534757 true] [17592186307954 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534757 true] [17592186307954 1041 "selection" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307954 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307955 13194139534757 true] [17592186307954 1110 #uuid "39d5bd33-5d17-5469-8cbe-cdc0b0c554f4" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307955 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307955 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307955 1111 #uuid "bd15d26e-bdf3-5fe0-84b9-980b25f01709" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307956 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/fcda8aaa1da0ee1e9fee4957246497a927b4373c51d8a46db429540ea51f5083.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307956 1111 #uuid "129832fc-dc2b-57ac-840e-63de2e90981a" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307957 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/cd35c57fa9c6ce3ae2ee5961b8ae3e7d16ea1a2aa44c302a9e40f19b6c1b11ea.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307957 1111 #uuid "1231c5af-cdb2-54d0-952b-de9c2a8060ed" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307958 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b6518f0f49f1147742d621fa940d49f84e7cce9c17d071fdfeb4a2e96abbf591.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307958 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307958 1111 #uuid "f6fe220a-a262-58d6-ac9b-4071188d639e" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307959 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/310ba014b846afb8d4f2bff610e762da71b47e8c4daa2d6ffebf4b62ecc4d1db.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307959 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307959 1111 #uuid "d217b59c-798f-51ff-abfb-5b1969eb6aa4" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307960 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307961 13194139534757 true] [17592186307960 1089 "q-181770" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307960 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534757 true] [17592186307960 1100 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307960 1113 #uuid "ff89cd3b-d498-586f-b4f1-6477eb383eeb" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307960 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/3fdfed680e3fb46adbe8a70aac86682aacb85d43e0675923f87658635fc89a1e.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7fbda500ecca7e18c9db9939ad78d4dcc6bf488bc7fc4de1137e764ce21a0254.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times R$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307962 13194139534757 true] [17592186307961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307963 13194139534757 true] [17592186307961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307964 13194139534757 true] [17592186307961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307965 13194139534757 true] [17592186307961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307966 13194139534757 true] [17592186307961 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534757 true] [17592186307961 1041 "selection" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307961 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307962 13194139534757 true] [17592186307961 1110 #uuid "ddff1d60-96cd-5249-ac1f-abfac8452187" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307962 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307962 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307962 1111 #uuid "311786a8-d33e-5f52-9723-a4f227c48979" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307963 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/95c7144f229cc34fcb98b1c1fa8bda8af8ca9dff68c7ae466e388ce3e525bc85.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307963 1111 #uuid "d8ab78d0-6e94-562a-bcc9-1e735e554629" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307964 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307964 1111 #uuid "3ae879f0-d017-5959-98dd-83863794ae80" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307965 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/43f665cebf4c3af1f1178d94b4ceec5d60bcfd4e61ebf955ed43cb603f8232b2.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307965 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307965 1111 #uuid "393ac199-32ce-5cdd-a7d3-8b36e3cc1bdb" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307966 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/eceb9c773d52b8bae73837252a885258b5a9b735cf3f24954b8755049d7cf07d.png" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307966 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534757 true] [17592186307966 1111 #uuid "c10aba9d-99f6-59ee-8505-e2aabf77a730" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307967 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307968 13194139534757 true] [17592186307967 1089 "q-181777" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307967 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534757 true] [17592186307967 1100 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307967 1113 #uuid "ef5d7cb6-447b-5fde-822f-0d2b0fd3c5e2" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307967 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times Z\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in Z}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8e677c814756c4818c25da450d789c974787df60cf4bb18017a96593d3ecc180.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f99beeb00c070048ee4ee7c1b6c9e077d7d4e0f3fd9e63aa12b5b58588e14dbb.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times Z & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times Z$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png)" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307969 13194139534757 true] [17592186307968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307970 13194139534757 true] [17592186307968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307971 13194139534757 true] [17592186307968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307972 13194139534757 true] [17592186307968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307973 13194139534757 true] [17592186307968 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534757 true] [17592186307968 1041 "selection" 13194139534757 true] [17592186307968 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307971 1319413953475